WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 17 Leader Video
Unit 5, Lesson 1: Miracles

1
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.900
So it's unit five, lesson one, and we get to talk about miracles.

2
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.440
Now, as you start to prepare for this week, I don't want you necessarily just

3
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.440
thinking about, well, I've got to prove to my students that miracles exist, because

4
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.300
we're going to kind of show a little bit of that.

5
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:30.740
But more so, that because there are miracles, it points to the

6
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:35.200
fact that God exists. And we start off, if you just look at what the students are

7
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.560
going in their Bible study, we actually chose 1 Kings 18 for that very reason.

8
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:46.020
Because when you look at that particular miracle, that miracle of Elijah and the

9
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.140
prophets of Baal and the huge fire that came down and ignited the sacrifice and how

10
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:56.640
that represented God's sovereign power in that miracle

11
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.500
is just another way we can see that amongst all the things that Jesus did and all

12
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.100
the things in the Bible, this is just a really, really cool example of how God

13
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.440
showed up and performed a miracle so that we can see exactly who God is.

14
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.660
As students are beginning this week and starting to read through, their intro

15
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:19.980
experience is going to walk them through what kind of, to get them starting to think

16
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.040
about miracles and what that looks like.

17
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.040
So we ask them specifically, like, what's the most improbable event in human

18
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.100
history? Just have them think through, like, gosh, what would be an improbable

19
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:36.860
event? So things like, it's not likely that if I stepped outside right now, I'd

20
00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.100
probably get struck by lightning.

21
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:44.340
Probably not going to happen. If I was to walk outside with a meteor

22
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.800
just sort of crash down on Earth and annihilate me and everybody around, probably

23
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.260
not going to happen. But is an improbable event the same as

24
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:59.260
a miracle? So the intro experience for the students is really to get them thinking

25
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.560
through when we talk about a miracle, what are we talking about?

26
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.920
Because you might get some answers.

27
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.840
When you start talking with your students, you might actually get some things like,

28
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.040
well, is it a miracle that when you're out shopping during Christmas season that a

29
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.320
parking spot right by the door opened up?

30
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.520
And is it a miracle and a sign from God that you get this parking?

31
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.820
Maybe, probably not. Is that a miracle, right?

32
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.440
Is it a miracle when a child is born?

33
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.460
We call called the miracle of birth, but is that really a miracle?

34
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.720
We're talking about miracles that we see in scripture.

35
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.080
So that's where you want your students to start to think through and get an idea of

36
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.380
what exactly we're talking about when we're talking about a miracle.

37
00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.160
So let's talk for a second about your storyteller.

38
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.420
Now it might have been difficult up to this point, and it may be difficult moving

39
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.340
forward to find the perfect person for your storyteller.

40
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.840
You might have had to search high and low, and maybe this is one of those spots

41
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.360
you're thinking, I really, I don't know exactly who I want for a storyteller.

42
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:09.360
For this week, I might suggest that you as a student find a student

43
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.920
that maybe has witnessed a miracle, maybe a miraculous healing.

44
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.960
I once had a student that it was hard to refute because she was such a trusted

45
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.800
and honest student, so we knew that she was telling the truth and her experience was

46
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.500
real, but she was on a missions trip in Africa.

47
00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.960
And she was praying over a young girl.

48
00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.600
And this young girl's legs were not the same somehow.

49
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.840
And she can't explain it and she would tell you today that she has no idea what

50
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.640
exactly happened or how it happened, but she knew some kind of miracle happened.

51
00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:51.580
But as they were praying, it felt to her like this little girl's leg grew

52
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:56.580
enough that they were the same. And she told me, she's like, Mr.

53
00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.480
Kozak, I don't know if this is real.

54
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.380
I don't know if this is true, but I feel like this happened.

55
00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:06.180
And she felt like she was a witness to an actual miracle, a little bit like what

56
00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.420
goes on in scripture. Now, none of us were there and none of us can verify it, but

57
00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:15.400
it was a really fascinating story that helps students see the power of a miracle

58
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.760
because what she thought happened, what she saw happen, deeply impacted her.

59
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.520
And today, she still regularly goes to Africa and serves as a missionary there on

60
00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.100
and off as much as she possibly can.

61
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.760
That moment deeply impacted her.

62
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.560
And I'm sure it impacted a lot of her fellow students.

63
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.320
So this is one of those times that it might be really cool to find a student that

64
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.820
maybe not that crazy of a story, but a story nonetheless that the student was a

65
00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:49.560
witness to a really divine real miracle so as you get ready

66
00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.280
to engage students in sort of a large group time I want you to kind of keep one

67
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.720
thing in mind that the misconception that we've given you that science can explain

68
00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.520
away miracles is a very real reality in the life of our students they're taught that

69
00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.320
they're conditioned to believe that they're conditioned really for that to be the

70
00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.880
default like instead of thinking right away something might be a miracle if you know

71
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.800
they're conditioned to think that right away something just can be explained by

72
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.720
science. And while there should be a healthy balance, because there's a lot of

73
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.960
things that we might think are miracles and end up being able to be explained, but

74
00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.720
there's a lot of things that we think we can explain but really end up actually

75
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.480
being a miracle and we really can't explain it as well as we think.

76
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.840
And science doesn't provide that kind of explanation.

77
00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.380
Science can't explain the supernatural.

78
00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:37.400
So be aware as you're preparing your lesson to figure out okay, how do I communicate

79
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.760
to students that miracles can actually happen and the difference between

80
00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.940
what science can explain and what science cannot explain?

81
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.380
It's going to compel students to start to think about, well, can miracles really

82
00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:55.600
happen today? And maybe more importantly, can miracles happen right here and right

83
00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.820
now? See, if you think about it, you're probably going to get some questions, and

84
00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.420
this may happen more in your small group time, but you're going

85
00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.360
that students will start asking, well, if miracles can happen, how come I don't see

86
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.840
miracles happening in my space and my culture, my sphere of influence and my family

87
00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.360
and those kinds of spaces in the same way that we saw miracles in scripture?

88
00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.780
I mean, if you just look at the very early stages of Acts and Peter and John walk up

89
00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.940
to the temple and there's a lame guy sitting in front of the temple.

90
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.020
He can't walk. He's never been able to walk.

91
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.480
And the Bible tells the story of Peter simply reaching his hand out saying, I don't

92
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:38.180
have any money, but what I have I give you in the name of Jesus.

93
00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.600
Get up and walk. And the guy gets up and walks and he's instantaneously healed.

94
00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.620
And what's fascinating about that story is that this guy has never been able to walk

95
00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:51.460
since birth. Complete muscle atrophy in his legs.

96
00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.040
And all of a sudden his ankles become strong and he's jumping up and down.

97
00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:59.400
He's walking. So students are going to ask the question, well, how come those

98
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.260
miracles don't happen here today?

99
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.640
Well, they might not happen in your city all that frequently, if ever.

100
00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.540
They might not happen in this country all that often or that frequently or whatever.

101
00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.720
But we don't know that they don't happen.

102
00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.980
They might. And it's likely that they do happen.

103
00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.980
Maybe not to that degree. I don't know.

104
00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.720
But we can't discount the fact that they just don't happen.

105
00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:27.920
We can't say they just don't happen.

106
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.600
They might happen. And God has the power to make that happen if he so chooses.

107
00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:39.060
So miracles of a biblical proportion, I would argue, are still possible.

108
00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:44.400
So while students are in small groups, encourage your small group leaders to really

109
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.260
take time this week to ask, maybe pry a little bit your students to see, are

110
00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.880
they really convinced? And convinced of a couple of things.

111
00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.980
First, are they convinced that miracles are even possible at all?

112
00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.900
Is that even a possibility? And take note of that.

113
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.840
Have your small group leaders actually take note of that because that's going to

114
00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.520
become important next week when we start talking about the resurrection of Jesus.

115
00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:13.040
Are your students convinced that miracles are at least possible?

116
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:19.440
Number two, are your students convinced that miracles have happened in their midst,

117
00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:23.420
whether by their friends or by people that they know or have they read things?

118
00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:26.840
Are they convinced that miracles have actually happened?

119
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.100
So yes, the first they have to be convinced that they're possible in order to be

120
00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:31.460
convinced that they've happened.

121
00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:33.860
But walk them through that process.

122
00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:39.880
And then ask them if they're not convinced, what would it take?

123
00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:46.020
What would it take to help them see the probability and the possibility of miracles

124
00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:50.120
all around them? Do they have to open up their view of God?

125
00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:54.820
Do they have to just open up their view of who Jesus is and what the cross is all

126
00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.080
about? We'll get to that next week.

127
00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:02.160
What has to happen for them, do they think, in order to make miracles a reality

128
00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.580
in their mind so that they can at least see some of the things that God is up to?

129
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.780
One thing to keep in mind as you're navigating through this, although we talk a lot

130
00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:15.240
about the fact that miracles are possible and they're verifiable and that they do

131
00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:19.660
happen, and we see throughout Scripture that God works in mighty ways and we want to

132
00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.900
boast that miracles happen all throughout the world.

133
00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:26.180
But this can also be a very, very personal thing.

134
00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:32.160
Miracles can bring up really, really important questions in the lives of our

135
00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:32.500
students.

136
00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:39.460
How come God performed a miracle in this person's life but not in my life?

137
00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.880
How come God healed this person but not this person?

138
00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.200
It almost feels like God is picking and choosing.

139
00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:53.960
How does that work, and how do you walk with students through

140
00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.720
difficult times like that? And I'm going to be honest with you.

141
00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.200
There's really not a specific answer that I can give you that, well, God does this

142
00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:07.000
because it's not there. But your job as a leader is to walk alongside

143
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:12.020
and help your students in their specific situations navigate those particular

144
00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:17.600
circumstances and those particular parts of their lives so that they grow closer

145
00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.280
to Christ, not further.

